# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Code coverage profiles and other test artifacts
*.out
coverage.*
*.coverprofile
profile.cov

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work
go.work.sum

# env file
.env

# Editor/IDE
# .idea/
# .vscode/

# macOS
.DS_Store

# Local migration output. Exports contain real tenant data, so keep them out of
# the repository even when they are written into the working directory.
exported_device_*.json
*_child_*.json
*_identities.json
id_mapping.json

# Per-device data folders written by `export --dataTypes`. Deliberately broad:
# the folder holds measurements, events, alarms and event attachments, and the
# attachments keep whatever name and extension the source event gave them, so
# there is no file pattern to match on.
data/

# Compressed exports written by `export --output-compressed`. The file name is
# chosen by the caller, so this covers the usual gzipped tar extensions.
*.tgz
*.tar.gz

# Test fixtures use the same file names as real export output on purpose, so
# they must be exempted from the rules above.
!tests/fixtures/**

# bats helpers cloned by `make bootstrap`
tests/vendor/

# The compiled Go binary. The Go template above only covers .exe/.so/.dylib, and
# a bare executable named after the repository is neither.
/bin/

# The microservice package: the image tarball and the zip the platform is fed,
# both produced by `make service-package`. The tarball alone is tens of MB.
/build/

# Release archives, from `make dist`.
/dist/

# The user interface plugin: its dependencies and its build output. node_modules
# for the Web SDK is most of a gigabyte.
ui/node_modules/
ui/dist/
ui/.angular/
